  q(ANTH,'Bones from several people are mixed together in one grave. What does the MNI tell investigators?','The minimum number of individuals present',['The exact age of each person','The cause of death','The time since burial in years'],'C','MNI is often found by counting the most common repeated bone, such as left femurs.',BELL),
  q(ANTH,'Why does a newborn have more bones than an adult?','Many bones are still separate and fuse together during growth',['Adults lose bones from disease','Babies have extra ribs','Adults lose about 60 bones to disease and injury over their lifetime'],'D','A newborn has about 270 bones, which fuse over time into the adult total of 206.',BELL),
  q(ANTH,'Bones found after a fire are white, brittle and cracked. What does this show?','They were burned at a very high temperature (calcined)',['They are very old','They were buried in clay','They were buried for centuries in dry, sandy soil without moisture'],'A','Calcined bone has lost its organic material and shrinks and warps from intense heat.',BELL),
  q(ANTH,'Bony lipping and spurs around the joints of a skeleton suggest what about the person?','They were likely an older adult with arthritis',['They were a young child','They were female','They died from poisoning'],'B','Degenerative joint changes increase with age and also with heavy physical use.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'What does an insect-based estimate of time since death usually give investigators?','A minimum postmortem interval',['The exact minute of death','The cause of death','The manner of death'],'C','Insects can arrive only after death, so the estimate shows the shortest time the body has been dead.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Where do blowflies usually lay their first eggs on a body?','In natural openings and open wounds',['On the soles of the feet','Under the fingernails','Only on clothing'],'D','Moist openings such as the eyes, nose and mouth give larvae easy access to food.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Which beetle family, called carrion beetles, feeds on dead animals and fly larvae?','Silphidae',['Calliphoridae','Sarcophagidae','Muscidae, the family of house flies'],'A','Carrion beetles arrive during active decay and can help estimate later stages of decomposition.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Rove beetles are found on a body eating maggots. What role do they play?','Predators of other insects',['The first colonizers','Necrophagous feeders on tissue','Insects that arrive only on dry bones'],'B','Predators and parasites can reduce the number of maggots and affect time-of-death estimates.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Why might an entomologist raise live maggots from a body to adulthood?','To identify the species more reliably from the adult fly',['To keep them as pets','To make them grow faster','To prove the maggots arrived before the person died, not after'],'C','Adult flies are easier to identify, and species-specific growth rates make the time estimate accurate.',BELL),
  q(BLOOD,'Small drops that land around a main drop after it hits a surface are called:','Satellite spatter',['Cast-off','Pooling','A void'],'D','Satellite drops form from the edges of the parent drop, especially when it falls on a rough surface.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'Analysts run strings from several stains at their impact angles. What three-dimensional point are they finding?','The area of origin',['The area of convergence','The point of exit','The first transfer stain'],'A','The area of origin adds height to the two-dimensional area of convergence on the floor or wall.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'Large stains in an up-and-down wave pattern on a wall suggest which source?','Arterial spurting from a damaged artery',['Blood dripping from a weapon','Expirated blood from the mouth','A swipe from a hand'],'B','Each pulse of the heart pushes blood out with force, creating the wave-like pattern.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'Which presumptive blood test turns green when blood is present?','Leucomalachite green',['Kastle-Meyer','Luminol','Takayama'],'C','Like other presumptive tests, it reacts with the peroxidase-like activity of hemoglobin.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'An object moves through an existing wet bloodstain and alters it. What is this pattern called?','A wipe pattern',['A swipe pattern','Cast-off','Satellite spatter'],'D','A wipe moves blood that was already there, while a swipe deposits blood onto a clean surface.',BERT),
  q(DNA,'Which marker in STR kits shows whether DNA came from a male or a female?','Amelogenin',['CODIS core locus D8','Mitochondrial DNA','Y-STR only'],'A','The amelogenin gene differs in length on the X and Y chromosomes, giving one or two peaks.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'In sexual assault cases, which method separates sperm cell DNA from the victim’s cell DNA?','Differential extraction',['Gel staining','Mini-STR testing','Mitochondrial sequencing'],'B','Sperm heads resist the first breakdown step, so the female and male DNA end up in separate fractions.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'How many core STR loci have been used for CODIS profiles since 2017?','20',['13','5','46'],'C','The number of core loci was raised from 13 to 20 to improve matching power and work with other countries.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'Which test uses shorter target regions to get results from badly degraded DNA?','Mini-STR analysis',['Standard RFLP','Paternity testing','Fingerprint analysis'],'D','Degraded DNA is broken into short pieces, so shorter targets are more likely to stay intact.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'Why does a DNA lab run a reagent blank alongside evidence samples?','To check that the chemicals are not contaminated with DNA',['To make the sample larger','To identify the suspect','To make the DNA profile from the evidence stronger and easier to read'],'A','A reagent blank should show no DNA; any result means contamination may have occurred.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'The Tarasoff case set what duty for mental health professionals?','A duty to warn or protect people threatened by a patient',['A duty to keep every secret','A duty to testify in all trials','A duty to report all minor crimes'],'B','When a patient makes a serious threat against an identified person, steps must be taken to protect them.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'How does an offender’s “signature” differ from the modus operandi (MO)?','A signature meets an emotional need and stays the same, while the MO can change',['They are the same thing','The MO is only used in court','The signature is always a written note left at the scene'],'C','The MO is how a crime is done and may improve over time, while the signature reflects personal needs.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'An offender kills four or more people in one location during a single event. What is this called?','Mass murder',['Serial murder','Spree murder','Manslaughter'],'D','Mass murder happens in one place at one time, while spree killers move between locations.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'Under the ALI (Model Penal Code) insanity standard, what is a defendant unable to do?','Appreciate the wrongfulness of the act or conform their conduct to the law',['Remember their own name','Read the charges','Hire a lawyer or understand the charges well enough to help with the defense'],'A','The ALI test is broader than M’Naghten because it also considers the ability to control behavior.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'When forensic interviewers talk with young child witnesses, which type of question is preferred?','Open-ended questions such as “Tell me what happened”',['Leading yes-or-no questions','Questions with the answer built in','Repeated questions until the child changes the answer'],'B','Children are easily influenced, so open-ended questions reduce the risk of false information.',BELL)
